00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:30.140
because most reporting tends to simplify it considerably. Most stories treat disputes over NATO

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:34.860
spending as a straightforward question of whether allies are paying their fair share. But the

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:41.200
often cited 2% of GDP target is a guideline rather than a binding legal obligation under any treaty.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:46.280
Different countries measure and report their defense spending using methodologies that can be difficult

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:51.360
to compare directly, which adds another layer of complexity. Here's how the system actually functions.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:57.480
NATO members agreed at the 2014 Wales Summit to work towards spending 2% of their gross domestic

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:04.120
product on defense by 2024. There is also a separate target that 20% of that defense spending should

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:10.880
go toward major equipment purchases. Importantly, there is no enforcement mechanism or automatic penalty

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.920
for failing to meet these targets. Instead, they function as political commitments and a form of

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.240
peer pressure benchmark. Each country self-reports its figures using NATO's agreed definitions,

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.680
which sometimes include items such as military pensions or certain infrastructure projects that

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.720
national budgets might classify differently. This creates space for both genuine increases in

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:37.060
capability and what some critics call creative accounting. The United States has historically accounted

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:42.480
for roughly two-thirds of total NATO defense spending, which gives Washington significant leverage in

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:47.560
these conversations, but also makes it particularly sensitive to any perception that it is carrying

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:53.160
a disproportionate share of the burden. Understanding these mechanics changes how we should interpret

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:58.480
news claims about who is meeting targets, or whether one leader successfully pressured others to spend

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:04.800
